# Oh My Darling, Clementine, strum and sing (repertoire plan: clementine). A new arrangement of
# the 1884 song. Your part: a waltz strum with two chords, D (xx0232) and A7 (x02020): the bass
# note alone on beat 1 (open string 4 under D, open string 5 under A7), downstrums on beats 2
# and 3. The tune starts with a pickup on beat 3 ("In a"), so you wait one beat and come in on
# "cav-". Every line of eight bars has the same chords: D D D A7 | A7 D A7 D.
# The tune (second track) is the 1884 melody, the same for verse and chorus, in first position
# on strings 5 to 3: do do | do sol, mi mi | mi do do mi | sol sol fa mi | re ..., with the low A
# (open string 5) and C# (string 5, fret 4) under the tonic D (open string 4).
# Words: the 1884 chorus and the first two verses only, as commonly sung ("You are lost and gone
# forever"; some old printings read "Thou art lost").
title: Oh My Darling, Clementine
id: clementine
composer: Traditional American (credited to Percy Montrose, 1884)
source: Song published in 1884; the 1884 tune and words (chorus, verses 1 and 2); arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain; this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 2
tags: strumming, waltz, 3/4, pickup, two chords, lyrics
tempo: 108
time: 3/4
key: D
pickup: q

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
